.TH XXD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
xxd \- make a hex dump, or reverse one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xxd
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ infile
.RI [ outfile ]]
.br
.B xxd \-r
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-s
.RI [\-] offset ]
.RI [ infile
.RI [ outfile ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xxd
writes a hex dump of
.IR infile ,
or with
.B \-r
converts a hex dump back to binary.
This
.B xxd
is rexxd, a reimplementation of the xxd distributed with Vim.
In common cases its output is identical, but details differ; see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
With no
.IR infile ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
.B xxd
reads standard input.
With no
.IR outfile ,
or when it is
.BR \- ,
it writes standard output.
A dump replaces the contents of
.IR outfile .
With
.BR \-r ,
.I outfile
is not truncated, so that a dump can patch an existing file in place.
.PP
Each line of the default dump is an offset, a colon, 16 octets in hex in
groups of 2, two spaces, and the same octets as text.
The offset has 8 hex digits, or 16 once it no longer fits in 8.
In the text column, printable ASCII appears as itself and every other
octet as a period.
.PP
Numeric arguments are decimal, hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or octal with a leading
.BR 0 .
.SH OPTIONS
Only the letter after the dash is significant in options that take no
argument, so
.BR \-ps ,
.BR \-postscript ,
and
.B \-plain
all mean
.BR \-p .
Options cannot be grouped:
.B \-au
is the same as
.BR \-a .
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-c8 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-a
Toggle autoskip.
A run of all-zero lines is shown as its first line followed by a line
containing a single
.BR * .
If the input ends with such a run, its last line is also shown, so that
.B \-r
restores the full length.
.TP
.B \-b
Dump bits instead of hex: each octet as 8 binary digits, 6 octets per
line.
Incompatible with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B \-C
Capitalize the variable names written by
.BR \-i ,
including the
.B _LEN
suffix.
.TP
.BI "\-c " cols
Write
.I cols
octets per line.
The default is 16, or 6 with
.BR \-b ,
12 with
.BR \-i ,
and 30 with
.BR \-p .
.TP
.B \-d
Show offsets in decimal.
Incompatible with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.B \-E
Show the text column as EBCDIC instead of ASCII.
.TP
.B \-e
Little-endian dump: reverse the octets within each group.
Groups default to 4 octets, and
.I cols
must be a multiple of the group size.
Incompatible with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI "\-g " size
Group
.I size
octets together.
The default is 2, or 1 with
.B \-b
and 4 with
.BR \-e .
A size of 0 puts each line in a single group.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-i
Write a C include file: an
.B unsigned char
array of the octets and an
.B unsigned int
holding the length.
The names come from
.BR \-n ,
or else from
.IR infile ,
with each character that cannot appear in a C identifier replaced by an
underscore, and
.B __
prepended to a name that starts with a digit.
Non-ASCII characters are kept, so the names may be Unicode identifiers.
When reading standard input without
.BR \-n ,
only the array elements are written.
.TP
.BI "\-l " len
Stop after
.I len
octets.
.TP
.BI "\-n " name
Use
.I name
for the
.B \-i
variables, exactly as given.
.TP
.BI "\-o " offset
Add
.I offset
to the displayed file positions.
.TP
.B \-p
Plain hex dump: hex digits only, 30 octets per line, with no offsets and
no text column.
With
.BR \-r ,
read a plain dump, ignoring everything except hex digits.
.TP
.BI "\-R " when
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
There is no color output.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse: convert a hex dump to binary.
Each input line is an offset, a colon, and hex octets, and two spaces
in a row end the octets, so the text column is ignored.
Lines without a valid offset are skipped.
The number of octets per line is detected from each line, so
.B \-c
is unnecessary.
Octets are written at the offset of their line, by seeking when
.I outfile
is a regular file.
Otherwise gaps are filled with zeros, and offsets cannot go backwards.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR [\fB+\fR][\fB\-\fR]\fIoffset\fR
Start at
.I offset
in the input:
.I offset
from the start, or
.BI \- offset
from the end.
When reading standard input,
.BI + offset
seeks relative to the current position, and with a named
.I infile
the
.B +
is ignored.
If the input cannot seek, a forward seek reads and discards input.
Displayed offsets include the seek.
.IP
When
.B \-s
follows
.B \-r
on the command line, it instead adds
.IR offset ,
which may be negative, to each line's offset.
.TP
.B \-u
Use uppercase hex digits.
The
.B 0x
prefixes written by
.B \-i
stay lowercase.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-x
Trace each open, read, write, seek, and exit on standard error, like
.BR strace .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line.
.TP
.B 2
The input could not be opened or read, or a
.B \-s
seek failed.
.TP
.B 3
The output could not be opened or written.
.TP
.B 6
Out of memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xxd hello.txt
00000000: 4865 6c6c 6f2c 2077 6f72 6c64 210a       Hello, world!.
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the last 32 octets of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xxd \-s \-32 program.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Embed a file in a C program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xxd \-i icon.png >icon.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Edit a binary file as text, then convert it back:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xxd data.bin >data.hex
$ vim data.hex
$ xxd \-r data.hex data.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the octet 0x90 at offset 0x1f of an existing file, leaving the rest
of the file unchanged:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo 1f: 90 | xxd \-r \- program.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert between binary and plain hex:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xxd \-p key.bin >key.txt
$ xxd \-r \-p key.txt key.bin
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from Vim's xxd:
.IP \(bu 2
Text output ends lines with LF, not CRLF.
.IP \(bu 2
Standard output is always fully buffered.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no color output.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-r
detects the number of octets per line.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-n
names are used as given.
.IP \(bu 2
Offsets are 64-bit, and limits such as the maximum
.B \-c
come only from memory.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-x
is new.
.PP
Vim's own xxd manual does not describe this program.
.PP
Paths may contain any Unicode characters and may be longer than
.BR MAX_PATH .
Memory is limited to 16 MiB, and requests beyond that, such as a huge
.BR \-c ,
fail with exit status 6.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hexdump (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR vim (1)
